## Your rights at work

OCD can count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life. Your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- **extra time** for tasks affected by checking
- **flexible start times** if compulsions make mornings hard
- time off for **therapy**
- a quieter or more private workspace

**Access to Work** offers a free **Mental Health Support Service**.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how OCD affects you on **most days**. It is about whether you can do things **reliably**, which includes **in a reasonable time**. OCD can affect:

- **washing and bathing:** if washing rituals take many times longer than usual
- **dressing:** if you get stuck in repeated routines
- **preparing food:** if contamination fears or checking make it very hard
- **planning and following journeys:** if checking or fears stop you going out
- **mixing with other people:** if obsessions cause overwhelming distress

Say how long tasks take you, and what happens if you are interrupted.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you cannot work because of your mental health, you may be able to get **Universal Credit** or **New Style Employment and Support Allowance**.
